; =====================================================================
; dcbor-head.derived.abnf
;
; DERIVED - not verbatim.  draft-mcnally-deterministic-cbor-18 contains
; no ABNF at all; its only grammar notation is one quoted CDDL prelude
; rule (cddl-prelude-number.cddl).  Every rule below was written for this
; record from the source's prose and tables, and must never be quoted as
; the source's own grammar.
;
; Derived from draft-mcnally-deterministic-cbor-18:
;   Section 2.1    definite-length items only, for byte strings, text
;                  strings, arrays and maps - encoder MUST only emit,
;                  decoder MUST reject indefinite length
;   Section 2.6    only false (0xf4), true (0xf5), null (0xf6) and the
;                  floating point values are valid in major type 7
;   Section 2.5    the three float widths are all retained (the reduction
;                  rules constrain which one, not whether)
;   Section 8.2    (non-normative) why `undefined` is excluded
;   Section 8.4    (non-normative) why the other simple values are
; and, for the frame this narrows, from RFC 8949 (STD 94):
;   Section 3      the initial byte, the argument and the head; argument
;                  widths 0/1/2/4/8; reserved additional information
;                  28, 29, 30
;   Section 3.1    Table 1, the major types
;   Section 3.2    Table 2, the indefinite-length heads; 3.2.1 "break"
;   Section 3.3    Tables 3 and 4, major type 7 and the simple values
;
; The language is ABNF (RFC 5234).  Terminals are single bytes.  OCTET is
; the RFC 5234 core rule and is used, not redefined.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCOPE.  This grammar covers exactly the *head* of a data item - the
; initial byte plus the bytes consumed to build the argument - in the
; sense RFC 8949 Section 3 gives the word.  It cannot cover a whole data
; item: the content length of a string and the number of enclosed items
; of an array or map are given by the argument just parsed, a
; context-sensitive dependency no ABNF expresses.  Nesting, map arity and
; "no bytes left over" are decided by Figure 1 of RFC 8949 Appendix C and
; by dcbor-validate.derived.pseudocode.txt.
;
; WHY IT EARNS ITS PLACE.  Two of dCBOR's seven narrowing rules are
; decidable from the initial byte alone, and this is the only artifact in
; this directory that decides them by construction rather than by a
; procedure: Section 2.1 (no indefinite length) and Section 2.6 (no
; simple value other than false, true, null).  The division against the
; CDDL is exact.  Section 2.1 is invisible in the generic data model -
; RFC 8949 Section 2 hides the serialization variant on purpose - so
; dcbor-profile.derived.cddl cannot express it at all.  Section 2.6 that
; file does express, at the data-model level; what it cannot do is check
; it on encoded bytes, which is how 0xf7 and 0xf8 arrive.
;
; THE CENSUS, which is the checkable claim this file makes.  Of the 229
; initial bytes that are well-formed under RFC 8949, dCBOR admits 202.
; The 27 it excludes are exactly:
;   5f 7f 9f bf   the four indefinite-length heads     (Section 2.1)
;   ff            the "break" stop code, which only an indefinite-length
;                 item can need                        (Section 2.1)
;   e0-f3         simple values 0 through 19           (Section 2.6)
;   f7            `undefined`                          (Sections 2.6, 8.2)
;   f8            the one-byte simple value head, i.e. simple values
;                 32-255                               (Sections 2.6, 8.4)
; Beyond those four indefinite-length heads, nothing is excluded in major
; types 0-6: each admits 28 initial bytes, which is all 28 that are
; well-formed for major types 0, 1 and 6, and 28 of the 29 well-formed
; for major types 2-5 (the one lost is the indefinite-length head itself).
; Everything dCBOR still asks of those types is a predicate over the
; *value* (Section 2.2 preferred serialization, Section 2.5 DCBOR_INT)
; and not over the initial byte.
;
; WHAT IT DOES NOT DECIDE.  Everything value-dependent, which for dCBOR
; is most of the document.  It enumerates the five legal argument widths
; but cannot require the shortest one that carries the value (Section
; 2.2); it admits 1b-headed negative integers but cannot see that the
; argument must stay below 2^63 (Table 4); and it admits f9/fa/fb but
; cannot tell a canonical NaN from a payload NaN, a reducible 42.0 from
; an irreducible 1.5, or a preferred f97c00 Infinity from a rejected
; fa7f800000.  Those are numeric-reduction.derived.pseudocode.txt.
; =====================================================================

; A dCBOR head.  Note the absence of any `break` rule and of any
; indefinite-length alternative: Section 2.1 removes both, so this
; grammar has no counterpart to RFC 8949's head-or-break.
dcbor-head = uint-head / nint-head / bstr-head / tstr-head
           / array-head / map-head / tag-head
           / dcbor-simple-head / dcbor-float-head

; --- major types 0 and 1: integers (RFC 8949 Section 3.1) ------------
; Unrestricted at head level.  Section 2.2 requires the shortest of these
; five forms that carries the value, and Table 4 bounds the major type 1
; argument by 2^63 - 1; neither is expressible here.
uint-head = %x00-17 / %x18 arg1 / %x19 arg2 / %x1a arg4 / %x1b arg8
nint-head = %x20-37 / %x38 arg1 / %x39 arg2 / %x3a arg4 / %x3b arg8

; --- major types 2 and 3: strings (Section 2.1) ----------------------
; 0x5f and 0x7f are absent by construction: the indefinite-length byte
; and text string heads of RFC 8949 Table 2.
bstr-head = %x40-57 / %x58 arg1 / %x59 arg2 / %x5a arg4 / %x5b arg8
tstr-head = %x60-77 / %x78 arg1 / %x79 arg2 / %x7a arg4 / %x7b arg8

; --- major types 4 and 5: arrays and maps (Section 2.1) --------------
; 0x9f and 0xbf are absent by construction, as above.
array-head = %x80-97 / %x98 arg1 / %x99 arg2 / %x9a arg4 / %x9b arg8
map-head   = %xa0-b7 / %xb8 arg1 / %xb9 arg2 / %xba arg4 / %xbb arg8

; --- major type 6: tags ----------------------------------------------
; Any tag number: the source places no restriction on which tags appear
; (Section 8.5 calls tags the escape hatch), only on the conformance of
; the content, which is a recursion this grammar cannot perform.  Tag 201
; of Section 3 and Section 6 is %xd8 %xc9 - one argument byte, not two,
; because 201 < 256 and Section 2.2 requires the preferred encoding of
; the tag number as much as of anything else.
tag-head = %xc0-d7 / %xd8 arg1 / %xd9 arg2 / %xda arg4 / %xdb arg8

; --- major type 7 (Section 2.6) ---------------------------------------
; The whole of dCBOR's narrowing of major type 7 is the shortness of
; these two rules.  Six of the 29 well-formed major type 7 initial bytes
; survive.
dcbor-simple-head = %xf4 / %xf5 / %xf6       ; false, true, null

; Section 2.5 retains all three widths and chooses between them by value:
; half for the canonical NaN 0xf97e00 and for the two infinities, and
; otherwise the shortest width that preserves the value.
dcbor-float-head = %xf9 arg2 / %xfa arg4 / %xfb arg8

; --- arguments (RFC 8949 Section 3, network byte order per 1.2) ------
arg1 = OCTET
arg2 = 2OCTET
arg4 = 4OCTET
arg8 = 8OCTET
